  "response": "`crimes context src/billing.ts` (run against `evals/fixtures/01-messy-ts-app`, which resolves to `examples/messy-ts-app`) plus a read of the file itself. Risk level: **high** — 8 findings (1 high, 5 medium, 2 low).\n\n## What the file does\n\n`src/billing.ts` is the invoice/billing domain module (311 lines, no imports — fully self-contained). It declares its own `User`, `InvoiceLine`, and `Invoice` interfaces and exports five things:\n\n- **`generateInvoice(user, items, paymentMethod?)`** — the centre of gravity, 204 lines (L50–253). It does *eight* unrelated jobs in one body: id/metadata generation, per-plan line pricing (`pro` 5% / `team` 10% / `enterprise` 20%, with a hidden `addon_*` SKU carve-out), loyalty discount by account age, inline promo-code resolution (`WELCOME10`/`WELCOME20`/`BLACKFRIDAY`/`REF_*`), inline fraud-risk scoring with allow/review/block, country tax, currency selection, PII audit logging to stdout, metric emission, email-body composition, and a fake retry loop.\n- **`calculateRefundPreview(user, invoice)`** — despite the name, it writes an audit record and sends an email before returning `invoice.totalCents`.\n- **`tax(country, subtotalCents)`** — the same country→rate table that's already inlined at L146–154 in `generateInvoice`.\n- **`recommendUpgrade(user)`** — 4-deep nested plan/tenure/country policy returning an upgrade string.\n- **`listRecentInvoices()`** — a stub returning `[]`.\n\nThe file's own header comment says it's an intentional fixture, not reference code.\n\n## Related files to read first\n\nFrom `related_files` and `likely_tests`, plus cross-file findings in the repo scan:\n\n| File | Why |\n|---|---|\n| `src/billing.test.ts` | the only test, and it's weak — one test with **zero assertions** (`\"renders the enterprise plan\"`), one with only a weak matcher (`\"creates an invoice\"`). Do not trust it as a safety net. |\n| `src/api/state.ts` | **most important.** Redeclares `User` and `Invoice`. Contract Split-Brain (high, conf 0.82): its `Invoice` is missing 6 fields billing declares — `createdAt`, `currency`, `discountCents`, `subtotalCents`, `taxCents`, `userId`; its `User` is missing `createdAt`. Nothing in the build checks the two against each other. |\n| `src/api/plan.ts`, `src/jobs/plan-sync.ts`, `src/ui/pricing.ts` | the `\"enterprise\"` literal is duplicated across these four files (billing at L7) — plan policy is scattered, so a pricing change here likely needs changes there. |\n| `src/routes/settings/billing.tsx`, `docs/billing.md` | share the billing domain; the route also has label drift (Plans/Billing/Subscription across nav, title, component). |\n| `src/date.ts` | same directory, and the natural home for the clock abstraction the date findings all point at. |\n\n## Risks flagged on this file\n\n**High**\n- **God Function** (conf 0.95) — `generateInvoice` L50–253, 204 lines, 3.4× the 60-line domain threshold. This is the one that makes every other edit here risky: the eight responsibilities have no seams, so touching one means re-validating all of them.\n\n**Medium**\n- **False Identity** (conf 0.80) — `calculateRefundPreview` L40–44 has a pure-sounding name but calls `saveRefundAudit` and `sendRefundEmail`. Any caller (or agent) that memoizes, reorders, or speculatively calls it will send real emails.\n- **Temporal Recklessness** (conf 0.90) — 7 clock reads at L57, 63, 92, 198, 238, 250, 273. The detector notes none of them decide a branch — they only record or render — so this is lower-risk than a branching clock read, but it still makes the file untestable without a fixed clock. (Note `Math.random()` at L56 is a second, unflagged non-determinism source in the same function.)\n- **Logic in the Alibi** (conf 0.74) — L39: *\"Only owners can refund enterprise plans unless billing support approves.\"* The code below it enforces nothing of the sort — no owner check, no approval check. That's a live authorization rule that exists only in prose.\n- **Commented-Out Corpse** (conf 0.90) — L33–37, a disabled legacy-refund block (`loadRefund` / `approveRefund` / `sendRefundReceipt`). Sitting directly above `calculateRefundPreview`, it's exactly the kind of thing an agent revives by mistake.\n- **God File** (conf 0.71) — 311 lines vs the 300 threshold. Marginal; the God Function is the real problem.\n\n**Low**\n- **Catch and Release** (conf 0.78) — L245–247, bare `catch {}` containing only a comment. The retry loop reports success regardless; the caller can't tell the email failed, and the error isn't even bound.\n- **Unfinished Business** (conf 0.70) — 4 markers (FIXME/HACK/XXX/TODO) at L183–308, incl. the \"should not log full PII in production\" FIXME at L183.\n\n## Guidance worth acting on\n\nThe tool's `agent_guidance` boils down to: read the whole file before editing, don't add more direct clock access, don't trust safe-sounding names, don't revive commented code, and propose the split as its own change rather than smuggling it into a feature edit.\n\nTwo things from `clues` that aren't findings:\n\n- **The suppression for this file is stale.** `.crimes/suppressions.json` suppresses `commented_out_code::src/billing.ts::` — but `matches_current_finding: false`, because the current fingerprint carries a discriminator (`::42a4bbb39f1f`). The finding is surfacing despite the entry. If you intended that comment block to stay quiet, the suppression needs re-pinning.\n- **Test gap is bottom-quartile** (raw 0.5, percentile 0) against modest churn (4 commits in 90 days, 1 author, last touched 2026-05-17). Low-traffic file, near-zero test cover.\n\nPractical order if you're editing: fix the duplicated tax table (L146–154 vs `tax()`) or the `Invoice`/`User` split-brain **first** — both are silent-divergence bugs. Rename `calculateRefundPreview` before anyone builds on the wrong assumption. Save the `generateInvoice` extraction for a standalone change, and write real assertions in `billing.test.ts` before you attempt it, because right